ROOT CAUSE FOUND IN THE CURRENT BUILD.

Do not change updateObj, syncXform, renderer reset logic, scale maths, or
rotation maths yet.

The new Scene toolbar acts on:

    selIds

but viewport picking currently updates only:

    selId

Therefore clicking a model in the viewport can produce:

    selId   = "object123"
    selIds  = Set(0)

The model appears selected and the OLD direct-drag path still moves it, but:

    selIds.forEach(...)

inside the toolbar executes ZERO times.

This perfectly explains why the user can perceive MOVE as working through
viewport dragging while ROTATE/SCALE +/- do nothing.

==================================================
1. CONFIRMED CURRENT CODE
==================================================

Scene viewport picking currently has:

    if (hits.length) {
        const hid = hits[0].object.userData.gid;
        selRef.current = hid;
        setSelId(hid);
        dragMode = e.shiftKey ? "dragY" : "drag";
        dragId = hid;
        ...
    }

It does NOT update:

    setSelIds(...)

The toolbar, however, does:

    selIds.forEach(id => {
        ...
        updateObj(...)
    })

So the two selection systems are out of sync.

==================================================
2. FIX VIEWPORT SELECTION FIRST
==================================================

On a normal viewport click of a model, make the selection state consistent:

    selRef.current = hid;
    setSelId(hid);
    setSelIds(new Set([hid]));

That should be the default plain-click behaviour.

Do not leave selId and selIds representing two different selections.

==================================================
3. ADD CTRL/CMD MULTI-SELECT CORRECTLY
==================================================

Since Scene already supports multi-select in the layer list, viewport picking
should follow the same conceptual rules.

Plain click:

    selId = hid
    selIds = { hid }

Ctrl/Cmd click:

    toggle hid in selIds

Suggested behaviour:

    setSelIds(prev => {
        const next = new Set(prev);
        if (next.has(hid)) next.delete(hid);
        else next.add(hid);
        return next;
    });

Then decide active anchor explicitly:

If adding:
    setSelId(hid)
    selRef.current = hid

If removing the active anchor:
    choose another remaining selected id or null

Do not leave:

    selId points to an object no longer in selIds.

